<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/acids-bases-and-ph">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Vinegar stings a cut, soap feels slippery, lemon juice dissolves limescale, bleach strips
    dye — four everyday substances, one underlying axis. The axis is the hydrogen ion: an
    acid, in the definition that finally stuck (Brønsted and Lowry, 1923), is a
    <strong>proton donor</strong> — it releases H⁺ ions in water — and a base is a
    <strong>proton acceptor</strong>. Every acid–base reaction is a proton changing hands,
    and nearly every familiar sharp taste (acids) and slick feel (bases) is that handover
    happening on your tongue or skin. Acids donate H⁺ ions; bases accept them — and water
    itself does both, very slightly: in pure water, H⁺ and OH⁻ each sit at 10⁻⁷ mol/L, so
    the neutral point is pH 7. Because real concentrations span 14 orders of magnitude, from
    battery acid to drain cleaner, chemistry reports them as <strong>pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]</strong>
    — one digit per tenfold change in strength, a compressed ruler for an enormous range.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lesson covers the full practical territory: what acids and bases characteristically
    do, how their oxides classify, how salts are prepared and purified in a lab, and the
    techniques — titration, chromatography, separation, ion testing — by which all of it is
    measured. The unifying thread is that acid–base chemistry is the syllabus's stoichiometry
    made visible: counting protons with colours instead of counters.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pH scale is a log ruler — each unit down is a tenfold acidifier" widget="plotter" config={phCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve from right to left: lemon juice (pH 2) has 100,000× the H⁺ of neutral
    water; oven cleaner (pH 13–14) runs the other way. Your blood holds pH 7.35–7.45 —
    drifting 0.4 beyond either edge is life-threatening, which is why blood carries
    CO₂/bicarbonate buffers (a later lesson's equilibrium in action).
  </p>

  <h2>The characteristic properties of acids and bases</h2>
  <p>
    Acids announce themselves by a fixed set of reactions, each one a proton handover with a
    by-product you can see or hear. Acids react with <strong>metals</strong> above hydrogen
    in the reactivity series to give a salt plus hydrogen gas (Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ — the
    fizzing is the test); with <strong>carbonates</strong> to give a salt plus water plus
    carbon dioxide (the classic marble-chip-in-acid effervescence, which also limewaters
    milky); and with <strong>bases</strong> in neutralisation to give a salt plus water
    only. They turn blue litmus red and shift universal indicator through the warm end of
    the rainbow. <strong>Bases</strong> (metal oxides and hydroxides; the soluble ones are
    alkalis) do the mirror image: they accept protons, neutralise acids, turn red litmus
    blue, and feel slippery because they saponify the fats of your skin — which is exactly
    why they are also dangerous.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction beginners must internalise: <strong>strong vs concentrated are
    different axes</strong>. Strength is about the fraction of molecules that donate:
    hydrochloric acid is a <em>strong</em> acid (essentially every molecule ionises in
    water) but can be very <em>dilute</em>; acetic acid in vinegar is a <em>weak</em> acid —
    only about 1% of its molecules donate at any moment — even poured at full strength.
    Strength is chemistry; concentration is arithmetic. A dilute strong acid can have a
    lower pH than a concentrated weak one, and safety rules treat them differently.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "all acids are dangerous and all bases are gentle." pH 1
    acid and pH 14 base are both capable of destroying tissue; concentrated sulfuric and
    concentrated sodium hydroxide are equally feared in a lab. Corrosiveness depends on both
    pH and chemistry, not on which side of 7 a solution sits.
  </p>

  <h2>Oxides: the periodic table sorted by proton taste</h2>
  <p>
    Burn an element and what is left behind — its oxide — reveals which side of the
    acid–base line the element lives on, and the answer sweeps the periodic table.
    <strong>Metal oxides</strong> (Na₂O, CaO, MgO) are <strong>basic</strong>: they react
    with acids and, if soluble, form alkaline solutions (CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂, limewater).
    <strong>Non-metal oxides</strong> (CO₂, SO₂, P₄O₁₀) are <strong>acidic</strong>: they
    react with bases and dissolve in water as acids (CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃ — the reason rain is
    naturally slightly acidic; SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ — the mechanism of acid rain from burning
    sulphur-rich fuels). In between sit the <strong>amphoteric</strong> oxides — ZnO and
    Al₂O₃ — which react with <em>both</em> acids and bases, a useful fingerprint for
    identifying them; and a handful of <strong>neutral</strong> oxides (CO, N₂O) that react
    with neither. The pattern is a genuine trend: metal character falls and acidic-oxide
    character rises left to right across a period, so a single row (Na₂O → Al₂O₃ → SO₃) is
    a tour from basic to amphoteric to strongly acidic.
  </p>

  <h2>Preparation of salts</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>salt</strong> is what remains when an acid's H⁺ is replaced by a metal ion or
    ammonium: acid + base → salt + water is the parent reaction, and the practical question
    is always "which starting materials and which purification route?" The method is chosen
    by the salt's <strong>solubility</strong>, so first the solubility rules worth knowing
    cold: all sodium, potassium and ammonium salts dissolve; all nitrates dissolve; most
    chlorides dissolve (except silver and lead); most sulfates dissolve (except barium and
    lead); most carbonates and hydroxides do not.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a <em>soluble</em> salt (say, copper sulfate), react the acid with an insoluble
    base — warm dilute sulfuric acid with black copper oxide, stir until no more dissolves
    — then <strong>filter off</strong> the excess solid and <strong>evaporate</strong> the
    blue solution to crystallisation. For an <em>insoluble</em> salt (say, barium sulfate),
    mix two soluble solutions whose ions recombine into the wanted solid — barium chloride
    plus sodium sulfate — and the precipitate that forms is filtered, washed and dried:
    this is <strong>precipitation</strong>. (The same reaction, with the patient's own
    barium meal, is why the gut lights up on an X-ray: barium sulfate is the insoluble
    exception.) Alkali + acid for soluble sodium/potassium salts completes the trio, and
    because both solutions are colourless and the endpoint invisible, it needs the
    technique of the next section.
  </p>

  <h2>Experimental techniques: how it all gets measured</h2>
  <p>
    Chemistry is measurement, and the syllabus packages the core techniques here.
    <strong>Experimental design</strong> means choosing apparatus and method that isolate one
    variable at a time: fixed volumes, controlled temperature, a single measured outcome —
    and repeats for reliability. <strong>Acid–base titrations</strong> are the flagship: run
    the unknown acid into a measured volume of base (or vice versa) until an indicator —
    phenolphthalein, methyl orange — flips colour, then use moles = concentration × volume
    on both sides of the balanced equation. Worked example: 25.0 cm³ of NaOH at 0.10 mol/dm³
    is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of HCl. Moles of NaOH = 0.025 × 0.10 = 0.0025; the equation
    HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O is 1:1, so moles of HCl = 0.0025; concentration =
    0.0025 ÷ 0.020 = 0.125 mol/dm³. Stoichiometry with a colour signal — and the reason
    burettes read to 0.1 cm³.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Separation and purification</strong> is the physical toolbox: filtration for
    insoluble solid from liquid; evaporation and crystallisation for dissolved solids;
    simple distillation to collect the solvent (seawater → water + salt) and fractional
    distillation to separate two liquids by boiling point (industrial ethanol, crude oil);
    paper chromatography to separate dissolved dyes by how strongly they cling to the
    stationary paper versus travel with the moving solvent — the R<sub>f</sub> value
    (distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent) is a substance's fingerprint,
    which is how ink forgery and doping tests begin. Finally, <strong>identification of
    ions and gases</strong> is qualitative analysis by chemical tell: flame tests (sodium
    yellow, potassium lilac, copper blue-green), sodium hydroxide precipitates (Fe³⁺ gives
    rust-brown, Cu²⁺ pale blue), silver nitrate for halides (chloride white, bromide cream,
    iodide yellow), and for gases — hydrogen squeaks with a lit splint, oxygen relights a
    glowing one, CO₂ turns limewater milky, ammonia turns damp red litmus blue.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked preparation, end to end</h2>
  <p>
    Assemble the preparation and identification toolkit into one practical: making
    magnesium sulfate ("Epsom salts") from insoluble magnesium oxide. Warm dilute
    sulfuric acid; add MgO a spatula at a time, stirring, until no more dissolves — the
    excess solid proves the acid has all reacted. Filter to remove the leftover oxide;
    the filtrate is magnesium sulfate solution. Evaporate about a third of the water,
    then leave the rest to cool slowly: large crystals grow as the solution passes its
    saturation point. Filter, dry between filter papers, done — acid + insoluble base →
    salt + water, executed as glassware. Compare the indicator problem for the alkali
    route: neutralising NaOH with HCl has no visible endpoint (both solutions clear), so
    you first run a trial with phenolphthalein to find the neutralisation volume, then
    repeat <em>without</em> indicator (or with the minimum) to produce the actual salt
    solution — a small piece of experimental design, the syllabus's 12.1 in action:
    trial run, controlled volumes, repetition for reliability, and a record of which
    error source (burette reading, endpoint overshoot) dominates the tolerance.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The pH number hides an equilibrium. Water auto-ionises: 2H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻, with the
    product of the two concentrations fixed at 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C (the constant
    K<sub>w</sub>). That single constraint is why pH 7 is neutral (H⁺ = OH⁻ = 10⁻⁷), why
    the two scales mirror each other (pH + pOH = 14), and why the neutral point
    <em>drifts with temperature</em> — pure water at 100 °C is pH ≈ 6.14, still neutral,
    because K<sub>w</sub> has moved. Weak acids add a second layer: their ionisation is an
    equilibrium with a constant K<sub>a</sub>, whose logarithmic twin pKa is the standard
    measure of acid strength used across biochemistry — the pKa of an amino acid's side
    chain decides what shape a protein takes at pH 7.4. Buffers work here too: a weak acid
    plus its conjugate base absorbs added H⁺ or OH⁻ in near-zero-net sum, which is how
    blood (CO₂/bicarbonate, plus proteins), seawater (carbonate) and shampoo (citrate)
    hold their pH against insult.
  </p>
  <p>
    The planetary case is the ocean. Since industrialisation the sea's surface pH has
    shifted from about 8.2 toward 8.1 — a 0.1 unit drop, which on the log ruler is
    <strong>30% more H⁺</strong>. The added CO₂ dissolves as carbonic acid, and the
    resulting carbonate chemistry makes it harder for corals, shellfish and plankton to
    build calcium-carbonate shells — quietly dissolving some pteropod shells outright.
    Acid rain tells the same log-scale story on land: natural rain is pH ≈ 5.6 (dissolved
    CO₂); polluted rain dips to 4 — twenty-five times more acidic, enough to acidify lakes
    and strip forest soils, and the reason SO₂ scrubbers on power-station chimneys were
    among environmental regulation's clearest wins. One ruler, fourteen orders of magnitude,
    two planetary-scale consequences — the compression is the point.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brønsted &amp; Lowry (1923); OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Acid–Base Equilibria); Cambridge IGCSE Chemistry syllabus (units 7 &amp; 12); NOAA ocean acidification data.</p>
